Today we're going deeper into strings. Strings are everywhere in software development: usernames, passwords, API responses, JSON data, logs, search engines and text processing.
1. Understanding String Immutability
A string is an immutable sequence of characters.
Immutable means you cannot change individual characters after creating a string.
name = "Python"

name[0] = "J"  # TypeError
Why?
Because Python doesn't allow direct modification of an existing string.
How do we modify strings, then?
We create a new string.
name = "Python"

name = "J" + name[1:]

print(name)
output:
Jython
Notice the distinction:
•	We didn't modify the original string.
•	We created a new string and assigned it to name.
This is an important concept when understanding Python's object model.
2. String Indexing and Slicing
Strings behave similarly to lists when accessing characters.
language = "Python"
Character	P	y	t	h	o	n
Positive index	0	1	2	3	4	5
Negative index	-6	-5	-4	-3	-2	-1
Examples:
print(language[0])     # P
print(language[-1])    # n
print(language[1:4])   # yth
print(language[::-1])  # nohtyP
Slicing syntax:
string[start:end:step]
Remember:
•	start is included.
•	end is excluded.
•	step determines the interval.
3. Important String Methods
Python provides several built-in methods for processing strings.
Let's examine the ones you'll use frequently.
Case conversion
text = "hello python"

print(text.upper())
print(text.lower())
print(text.title())
print(text.capitalize())
print(text.swapcase())
output:
HELLO PYTHON
hello python
Hello Python
Hello python
HELLO PYTHON    
Method	Function
strip()	Removes whitespace from both ends
lstrip()	Removes whitespace from the left
rstrip()	Removes whitespace from the right
Searching and replacing
text = "I love Java"

print(text.find("Java"))
print(text.count("a"))
print(text.replace("Java", "Python"))
output:
7
2
I love Python
find() returns the starting index of the first occurrence. If the substring isn't found, it returns -1.
index() is similar, but raises ValueError when the substring isn't found.
4. Checking String Content
Python has methods that help determine what kind of characters a string contains.
text = "Python123"

print(text.isalpha())
print(text.isdigit())
print(text.isalnum())
output:
False
False
True
Why?
•	isalpha() → All characters must be alphabetic.
•	isdigit() → All characters must be digits.
•	isalnum() → All characters must be alphabetic or numeric.
Other useful methods:
Method	Checks
islower()	All cased letters are lowercase
isupper()	All cased letters are uppercase
isspace()	All characters are whitespace
istitle()	String follows title-case rules
startswith()	String begins with specified text
endswith()	String ends with specified text
email = "student@gmail.com"
print(email.startswith("student"))
print(email.endswith(".com"))
               both return true
5. The split() Method
This is particularly important when processing user input and text files.
split() converts a string into a list.
sentence = "Python is powerful"

words = sentence.split()

print(words)
output:
['Python', 'is', 'powerful']
You can specify a separator:
data = "Pramod,18,CSE,8.5"

result = data.split(",")

print(result)
output:
['Pramod', '18', 'CSE', '8.5']
6. The join() Method
join() does the opposite of split().
It combines strings from an iterable into a single string.
words = ["Python", "is", "powerful"]

sentence = " ".join(words)

print(sentence)
output:
Python is powerful
Other examples:
words = ["2026", "11", "22"]

print("-".join(words))
print("".join(words))
print("/".join(words))
output:
2026-11-22
20261122
2026/11/22
Split vs Join
split()	join()
String → List	Iterable of strings → String
Breaks text apart	Combines text
"a,b".split(",")	",".join(["a","b"])
7. String Formatting — F-Strings
name = "Pramod"
age = 18
cgpa = 8.5678

print(f"My name is {name}.")
print(f"I am {age} years old.")
print(f"My CGPA is {cgpa:.2f}.")
output:
My name is Pramod.
I am 18 years old.
My CGPA is 8.57.
 The expression {cgpa:.2f} formats the number to two decimal places.
More examples:
number = 1250000

print(f"{number:,}")
print(f"{number:010}")
print(f"{12.5:.1f}")
output:
1,250,000
0001250000
12.5
8. Character Frequency — An Important Problem-Solving Concept
Suppose you have:
text = "banana"
You want to count how many times each character appears.
One way is to use a dictionary.
text = "banana"

frequency = {}

for char in text:
    if char in frequency:
        frequency[char] += 1
    else:
        frequency[char] = 1

print(frequency)
Output:
{'b': 1, 'a': 3, 'n': 2}
Let's understand the logic:
1.	Start with an empty dictionary.
2.	Visit each character.
3.	Check whether the character already exists.
4.	If it exists, increase its count.
5.	Otherwise, create it with a count of 1.
This pattern is useful in text analytics, data processing and algorithmic problem-solving.


